<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # <span style="color: #d35400;">It's My Family!</span> – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family and I</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's My Family!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Speaking: Productive: Locate objects and people using words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Interactive: Respond nonverbally to simple yes/no questions by nodding or shaking head.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Speaking: Can identify family members, using simple sentences (e.g., "She is my mom.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can answer basic yes/no questions about family members, using nods, gestures, or simple words (e.g., "Is she your mom?").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can participate in conversations about their family, using one-word responses (e.g., "Who is she?" "Mom.") or two-word responses (e.g., "Who is she?" "My sister.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of the lesson's target language and topic.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up: "Mystery Family Member"</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up large flashcards of family members (mom, dad, brother, sister) one by one, face down.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says, "Who is she/he?" and encourages students to guess aloud as a class.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students say "Mom!" or "Dad!" before the card is revealed.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a flashcard of a mom and says clearly, "She is my mom." focusing on the /m/ sound. Students repeat chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a flashcard of a dad and says, "He is my dad." focusing on the /d/ sound. Students repeat chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models simple sentences: "This is my mom. She is happy." or "This is my dad. He is tall."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a mom) "Is she a boy?" (Students shake head or say "No").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a sister) "Is she your mom or your sister?" (Students point to "sister" or say "sister").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the Scenario and Theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large poster or flashcards depicting a simple family tree with pictures and names (e.g., "Meet Tim's Family!"). A short, illustrated dialogue where Tim introduces his family members.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Introducing family members: Use simple sentences to describe family (e.g., "She is my mom."). Asking about family: Respond to basic questions about family members (e.g., "Who is he?" "He is my brother."). Describing Family Roles: Using appropriate language to describe family members and their roles (e.g., "This is my mother.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to each family member on the poster and says: "This is Tim. This is his mom. She is friendly." "This is his dad. He is tall." "This is his sister. She is young." (Using grammar: personal pronouns + verb, possessive adjectives, adjectives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads a short dialogue aloud, pointing to the characters in the poster:
                        <br>Teacher: "Hello, Tim! Who is she?" (points to mom)
                        <br>Teacher (as Tim): "She is my mom!"
                        <br>Teacher: "Who is he?" (points to dad)
                        <br>Teacher (as Tim): "He is my dad!"
                        <br>Teacher: "Is she your sister?" (points to sister)
                        <br>Teacher (as Tim): "Yes, she is my sister!"
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students repeat simple sentences after the teacher, chorally and individually: "She is my mom," "He is my dad," "She is my sister." (Focus on pronunciation: /m/, /d/, /s/).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to Tim's mom." (Students point on the poster/flashcards).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this Tim's brother?" (Teacher points to sister; students nod/shake head, or say "No, sister").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Focus on accuracy through controlled and semi-controlled activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: Personal pronouns + verb (She is, He is), Possessive adjectives (my mom, my dad), WH-questions (Who is she/he?).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: mom, dad, brother, sister, grandmother, grandfather, aunt, uncle, cousin, pet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: /m/ in "mom", /d/ in "dad", /b/ in "brother", /s/ in "sister".</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: "Listen and Repeat" (Controlled Practice)</b>
                        <br>The teacher shows family flashcards (mom, dad, brother, sister, grandmother, grandfather, aunt, uncle, cousin, pet). For each, students chorally repeat: "She is my [mom]." or "He is my [dad]." (Teacher ensures correct pronunciation of target sounds). The teacher then asks individual students to repeat.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: "Yes/No Family Check" (Semi-controlled Substitution Drill)</b>
                        <br>The teacher holds up a flashcard (e.g., sister) and asks a student, "Is she your mom?" The student practices responding with a nod/shake or simple words: "No, she is my sister." or "Yes, she is my mom." The teacher varies questions, using "Is he your dad?" for boy pictures.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: "My Family Picture Talk" (Pair Interaction)</b>
                        <br>Students work in pairs. Each student has their own pre-drawn family picture (from a previous lesson) or a set of family member picture cards. Partner A points to a family member in their picture and asks Partner B, "Who is she/he?" Partner B responds, "She is my mom." or "He is my brother." Then roles switch. Teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language in a realistic, communicative task, focusing on fluency.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Meet My Family!" Pair Share (Role-play/Presentation).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Meet My Family!"</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are paired up. Each student has their family drawing or a printout/template with blank spaces to draw/glue family members.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A shows their drawing/pictures to Student B and introduces at least three family members using sentences like: "This is my mom. She is friendly." "This is my brother. He is young." "This is my grandmother. She is old."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B listens and can ask simple questions: "Who is he?" or "Is she your sister?" Student A responds using simple words or sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After Student A finishes, they switch roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing fluency, use of target vocabulary and grammar, and encouraging interaction.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and student reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Meet My Family!" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students introduce at least two family members to a classmate and answer one simple yes/no question about a family member.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of family members (e.g., /m/ in "mom", /d/ in "dad", /b/ in "brother", /s/ in "sister").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "She is my..." or "He is my..." for introductions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can respond to "Who is she/he?" with a one- or two-word answer (e.g., "Mom", "My sister").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can respond nonverbally (nod/shake) or with "Yes/No" to "Is she your mom?".</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can say 'my mom' and 'my dad'." (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can tell a friend about my family." (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher and student reflection on the lesson, and connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can say 'my brother' and 'my sister'. Yes or No?" (Teacher asks, students respond with gestures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like talking about your family today?" (Students nod/shake head, or say "Yes/No").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., Students were engaged with the family pictures, pair work was effective for generating speaking practice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students need more one-on-one pronunciation practice; ensure clear visual cues are always present for early learners).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the specific objective and learning outcomes for speaking achieved? (Yes, students could introduce and respond to questions about family members, using simple words and gestures).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will use the same family vocabulary and simple sentences to begin writing about their families, describing family members using the learned pronouns and possessive adjectives.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>